 ## Overview
-Sphere Tracing with Signed Distance Functions (SDFs) is a ray-marching technique that renders implicit surfaces by iteratively advancing a ray along its direction by a distance equal to the value of the SDF at the current position [S1, S5, unofficial]. It solves the problem of rendering complex, procedural, or fractal geometry without explicit mesh topology by leveraging the mathematical guarantee that no geometry exists within the distance $f(\mathbf{p})$ of any point $\mathbf{p}$ [S50, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from [Uniform Sphere Tracing](4406-uniform-sphere-tracing.md) by using variable, geometry-aware step sizes rather than fixed intervals, and from [Coarse-to-Fine Sphere Tracing](4615-coarse-to-fine-sphere-tracing.md) by relying on the exact SDF value at each step rather than hierarchical mip-level sampling [S5, unofficial].
+Sphere Tracing with Signed Distance Functions (SDFs) is an image-order rendering technique that computes the intersection of a camera ray with implicit surfaces by iteratively stepping along the ray using distance estimates provided by the SDF [S1, S34, unofficial]. It solves the problem of rendering complex, procedural, or fractal geometry without explicit triangle meshes by leveraging the mathematical property that the SDF value at any point $\mathbf{p}$ is the shortest Euclidean distance to the nearest surface [S2, S5, S9643]. Unlike standard ray marching which uses fixed step sizes, sphere tracing uses the SDF value itself as the step size, guaranteeing that no geometry is skipped because the SDF guarantees no geometry exists within distance $d$ of the current point $\mathbf{p}$ [S50, unofficial]. It differs from [FBM Sphere Tracing with BVH Acceleration](1728-fbm-sphere-tracing-with-bvh-acceleration.md) by relying on analytical distance bounds rather than spatial acceleration structures for primitives, and from [Step Size and Termination Rules](4132-step-size-and-termination-rules.md) by using the specific "safe" step size derived directly from the SDF evaluation.
 
